the comedy Soap Dish. The

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:37.870
villain of the movie is the one
yelling No, no, no here in

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:42.580
what's happening is on a live
broadcast of the soap opera.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:47.520
characters come out and reveal
from a yearbook that in fact,

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:52.440
she's a transexual, as the
terminology was at the time, and

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:57.300
this is apparently such a
humiliating revelation that she

00:19:57.300 --> 00:20:03.900
goes running off stage and is
plot wise defeated in the movie.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:09.750
And then in the upper left hand
corner is Jim Carrey in Ace

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:16.260
Ventura: Pet Detective. And this
is the big reveal scene, where

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:23.730
again, a female character is is
revealed to be transgender. And

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:29.430
it is, it actually parodies the
crying game a little bit,

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:32.310
because one reaction that some
people have to the revelation is

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:36.690
that they throw up. And again,
you know, these portrayals are

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:43.680
examples of pretty negative
portrayals that were, most

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.830
people kind of went with the
flow in the early 90s. And

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:52.200
these, these movies, all three
of them were successful in their

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:57.030
own in their own right. And the
point that I want to make here

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:59.790
is if you put these things
together, his prejudice is

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:03.120
really bad. We're not just
talking about people saying rude

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:08.460
things. We're talking about real
consequences here, and want to

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:11.640
share some of that data with
you. So the Gay Lesbian and

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:15.690
Straight Education Network
reports that 75% of transgender

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:19.320
students feel unsafe at school
because of their gender

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:23.700
expression. And in the most
comprehensive gathering of data,

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:28.530
the US Transgender Survey in
2015, that transgender students

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:33.840
indicate 54% being verbally
harassed, 24% physically

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.910
attacked, 13% sexually
assaulted. Those are pretty

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:46.860
unfortunate statistics. There is
a meta analysis in the sense of

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.320
kind of actually a meta
literature review done by Zack

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:56.250
Marshall in 2019. That found
that between 2010 and 2014,

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:01.200
looking just at studies at
transgender, non binary and

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:05.340
gender diverse individuals that
there are 99 studies, or

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.520
articles exploring
discrimination, and

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:13.500
marginalization of transgender
people, another 47 documenting

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:21.720
violence and trauma. So a very
well documented phenomenon. In a

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:27.780
survey of just from last June
2020 1500, self identified

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:33.870
members of the LGBTQ community
62% of transgender Americans

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:37.290
reported facing discrimination
of some kind in the past year,

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:41.610
that would have been 2021. Two
thirds report that that

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:45.060
discrimination moderately are
significantly affected their

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:49.230
psychological well being with
roughly half recording moderate

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:55.500
to significant physical impacts
as well. So there's a lot of

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:58.740
prejudice in the media has
played a role in that. Well,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.760
let's, let's unpack that a bit
more. But before I get to that,

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:07.830
one last slide here, in 2019,
again, you know, less than two

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:13.290
years ago, both the Human Rights
Coalition and the arguably

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:17.460
nonpartisan American Medical
Association described violence

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:22.950
against transgender people as an
epidemic. And it's not all

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:26.100
stranger violence, there's a lot
of documentation on partner

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:30.390
violence against transgender
people as well, in this

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:34.260
particular study by the Williams
Institute at UCLA, found that as

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:39.330
many as 50% experienced that
violence continues. This is a

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:44.940
headline from May 2021, the US
hits record for transgender

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:50.430
killings. And they're actually
not one but two Wikipedia pages

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:56.100
that are devoted to trying to
keep up with documenting these

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:58.770
unfortunate killings.

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:05.500
Okay, so there's a problem here,
and the media plays a role. And

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:08.470
that's what I want to talk about
two ways in which the media

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:13.210
plays a role. The first is
through the way the news frames

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:16.750
issues. TJ Billard, who I
mentioned a little bit earlier,

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:21.280
did a study in 2016, where he
analyzed a number of different

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:25.960
dimensions of language used in
media coverage, and argued that

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.120
there are certain kinds of
language that are very

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:32.980
delegitimizing for the trans
community. misgendering would be

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:36.700
an obvious example. And that
that kind of language can

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:41.980
detrimentally impact both the
projected legitimacy of

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:46.600
transgender claims, particularly
political claims, and in general

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:51.370
public perceptions of the
transgender community. So how

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:55.840
stories are framed that involve
transgender people can play a

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:02.590
attitude, influencing role.
Barry Tadlock political

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:07.630
scientist did a study that
covered over about 20 years

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:10.240
worth of news coverage. He
looked primarily at these

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:16.600
newspapers that are some
regional, nationally read

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:22.510
newspapers, and found some
interesting things. Most

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:25.300
articles about transgender
people were highly

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:28.360
individualistic about this
transgender person or that

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:33.850
transgender person. And that is
processed by people very

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:39.070
differently. And that's
different than a systemic or a

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:43.060
policy oriented kinds of
orientation. In fact, systemic

00:25:43.540 --> 00:25:47.770
orientation only occurred in
1.7% of the articles that

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:54.190
Tadlock looked at. The dominant
frames that Tadlock found were

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:57.790
things like education, which
means educating the reader about

00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:02.710
transgender issues, equality
claims, Liberty claims, safety

00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:06.490
and security, which sometimes
refer to the safety and security

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:10.840
of transgender people. Sometimes
the safety and security fears

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:14.950
that people have transgender
people. Pathology, is as it

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:20.830
sounds, a negative frame that
describes transgender people as

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:25.270
having a pathological issue.
Tadlock found in about half of

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:30.220
the study studies, or articles
that she'd say, analyze, there's

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:34.090
actually pretty evident positive
or negative perspective being

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:38.830
proposed about half the time.
And of those 29 articles 10 of

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:45.550
29 were explicitly negative and
anti transgender rights. So the

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:51.340
media framing plays a role. Now
recall that Tadlock's analysis

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:57.550
ended in 2011, I think it would
be a good research project to

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:02.530
update that analysis. And if
anybody's interested in working

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:05.470
with me on that, otherwise, I do
know of some potential

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:11.530
collaborators across the river.
Anecdotally, I tend to notice in

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:17.920
the last year or so, two sort of
frames that, again, I've noticed

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:20.920
I can't claim empirically that
they're dominant, but one is

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:28.420
framing that clearly makes it a
statement that there are policy

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:33.820
proposals that are attacks on
transgender people. And this one

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:37.540
from CNN from April of this
year. He says this record

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.960
breaking year for anti
transgender legislation would

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:46.090
affect minors the most. So you
know, that positions transgender

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:51.940
people, as in this case, you
know, victims of policy assault,

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:58.330
if you will. The the alternative
framing that I think is also

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:07.210
pretty common this is this is
one from television is the more

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:13.420
typical, pseudo neutral frame of
It's a battle, right, the battle

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:19.330
over transgender rights, and
which case the news author is

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:24.250
ostensibly not on either side,
but is reporting that there's a

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:28.390
battle that kind of in that kind
of framing of course, equalizes

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:33.730
matters and normalizes the
conflict. So my working

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:37.600
hypothesis that I'd like to
explore empirically is whether

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:42.550
or not those in fact are
predominant frames or not.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:47.760
We also know is an interesting
study that that came out this

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:52.710
year. That framing impacts
transgender people as audience

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.440
members as well. And here you
see the headline for yourself.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:00.750
This is in a journal called LGBT
Health, negative transgender

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:04.380
related media message, our
messages are associated with

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:09.360
adverse mental health outcomes
and a multi state study of

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:13.350
transgender adults. In fact, in
some instances, that kind of

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:17.730
reaction was described as like
post traumatic stress, because

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:24.930
the coverage forced some viewers
to kind of relive prejudice or

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:28.050
attacks that they'd had to
experience themselves in the

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:34.890
past. The other way that media
can influence attitudes that

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:38.670
potentially I think is more
positive, is through

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:42.540
entertainment media. There's a
great deal of research, some of

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:46.200
which I'm going to talk about
here. That indicates that media

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.530
representation really does
matter. It's not only the if you

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:52.560
can see it, you can be it
coverage, which I think is also

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:56.100
important, but it can also
actually decrease prejudice

00:29:56.100 --> 00:30:00.990
towards specific social groups.
And remember that statistic I

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:03.900
said earlier were only 10% said
they know somebody who's

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.990
transgender. We know from the
research that I'm about to

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:10.470
describe that the the
entertainment media is effect is

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:15.270
the biggest, with those people
who don't have real world

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:21.330
contact with those groups. So,
to to get us there, I want to

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:25.170
quickly recall what's known as
the contact hypothesis that

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:28.830
dates back to the 1950s. It's
most often credited, although

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:31.290
you don't think he actually
originated it, but he certainly

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:37.740
popularized it, Gordon Alport.
And he believed and argues in

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:41.820
his his 1954, very, very
influential book on prejudice,

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:46.050
that prejudice is fueled by
ignorance, that we don't know a

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:50.040
social group, and therefore we
reduce them to the stereotypes

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:53.040
that we've heard about them that
are frequently false and

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:59.220
negative. So Alport helped to
popularize the contact

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.930
hypothesis that says contact
facilitates learning about a

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:07.290
social group. And if you the
more you learn about a group,

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:11.190
and recognize how diverse they
are, and that, you know, the

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:15.990
stereotypes are wrong, and
overgeneralize, then the more

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:23.010
you can reduce prejudice. Now,
there's been six decades, almost

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:27.030
seven, seven decades of research
on the contact hypothesis is

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:30.420
been supported by literally
hundreds of studies. And the

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:35.880
last meta analysis, I saw I
think, had over 600 studies that

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:39.240
have supported the contact
hypothesis. But in the process

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:42.300
of articulating it, there are
certain conditions that need to

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:46.980
be met for contact contact, to
have a beneficial prejudice

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:51.600
reducing effect, there needs to
be a perception of equal status.

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:55.140
That's why in the Deep South,
blacks and whites had a lot of

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:59.640
contact. But until the Civil
Rights legislation established

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:04.860
equal, more equal rights, you
know, Jim Crow laws were still

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:08.460
essentially reinforcing the
prejudicial attitudes that many

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:13.710
southerners had. It helps to
have common goals, it helps to

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:17.190
have situations where there can
be intergroup cooperation.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:21.540
Importantly, this next one
deserves sort of an extra star,

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:28.620
it needs to not be opposed by
authorities. And the law, which

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:33.270
again explains why in the south,
it took major legislation to

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:37.530
begin to break down the level of
prejudice and, and there of

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:41.280
course needs to be sufficient
contact both quantity and

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:43.710
quality wise to

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.020
permit people to learn about
groups and for their prejudice

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:55.330
to be reduced. A movie that
really nicely illustrates what

00:32:55.330 --> 00:32:58.150
I'm talking about here is a
movie called Remember The Titans

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:01.720
which is actually based on a
true story in Virginia, where

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:05.020
they, the first year they
integrated the football team.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:10.870
And both sides start off very
distrustful of the other. And

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:13.990
particularly the white students
were, you know, obviously in the

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:19.450
in majority group, and were many
of whom were pretty racist. But

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:23.650
the coaches eventually work
together and they create that

00:33:23.650 --> 00:33:28.030
situation where there was the
black and white players had to

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.670
room together at camp, they had
to learn about each other, they

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:33.850
had to report something that
they learned about their their

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.090
roommate. And then of course,
they had the common goals have

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:42.430
success in the game. And by the
end of the movie, the the two

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:47.410
featured characters, one white,
white one, black, had become

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:50.980
essentially best friends. And of
course, the team was very, very

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:54.580
successful. But what I like
about it in particular, other

00:33:54.580 --> 00:33:59.500
than it's based on a true story,
is that it it really does

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:03.400
illustrate the contact
hypothesis in the various

00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:08.980
conditions involved with it
being met. Yeah, as I just said,

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:17.410
It met these various conditions.
Now, what happened in about 2005

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:22.660
is the development of something
that my colleagues and I called

00:34:22.660 --> 00:34:27.130
the parasocial contact
hypothesis. Parasocial simply

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:30.220
refers to the fact that the
contact is not face to face, but

00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:34.120
as mediated. And there's already
a body of literature going back

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:37.180
to the 50s that talked about
parasocial interaction and

00:34:37.180 --> 00:34:42.100
parasocial relationships.
Hortman and Wohl turn that

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:47.290
phrase back in 1956. So what we
did was we basically smooshed

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:50.890
together the notion of
parasocial interaction with the

00:34:50.890 --> 00:34:55.150
contact hypothesis, and did a
series of I think we did a total

00:34:55.150 --> 00:35:00.220
of five or six studies that
found that people can learn

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:05.290
through that parasocial contact,
in ways that are analogous to

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:09.400
the contact hypothesis, face to
face learning experiences, and

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:15.400
it can reduce prejudice. So,
five of those studies were

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:19.330
published in, I guess it was
five studies. Communication

00:35:19.330 --> 00:35:22.990
Monographs, the 2005 article was
simply titled The Parasocial

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:27.040
Contact Hypothesis. In the
Journal of homosexuality, we

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:30.490
published a study specifically
on the TV show Will and Grace.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:34.870
And in my book Beyond
Representational Correctness, I

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:39.670
talked about a film class that I
talk that we found that watching

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:44.080
certain films decrease prejudice
towards sexual minorities. The

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:48.490
studies or the shows in the
communication monographs article

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:53.170
included Six Feet Under, and the
original iteration of the show

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:57.700
Queer Eye for the Straight Guy,
which was a great stimuli set of

00:35:57.700 --> 00:36:01.210
stimuli because there are five
different gay guys on the show.

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:04.510
So there was a lot of
opportunity for viewers who

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:08.830
might not know any game men in
real life, to learn a lot about

00:36:08.830 --> 00:36:14.200
that category. And this research
was actually the first empirical

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:18.310
research that documented that
prejudice could be reduced

00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:21.340
towards sexual minorities. And

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:27.510
yeah, so that's the parasocial
contact hypothesis. And again, I

00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:30.690
want to emphasize that the sort
of the amount of change depended

00:36:30.690 --> 00:36:33.960
on prior contact. So if you had
a person who already had two or

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:36.390
three gay friends, their
attitudes were not changed all

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:40.650
that much, if at all. But it was
that group that had little to no

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:44.700
real world contact, and
therefore you didn't know much

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:49.350
about other than stereotypes.
That is the group where we saw

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:54.600
the most dramatic decrease of
prejudice. And I love this quote

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:58.740
from Gordon Alport. A
differentiated category is the

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:03.300
opposite of a stereotype. So
when you learn that members of a

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:07.710
particular social group are
actually quite varied, it is a

00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:12.600
key cognitive step into breaking
down that prejudice. We actually

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:16.170
also included a study I forgot
this one this was on about Eddie

00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:24.240
Izzard, who is a actor and stand
up comic. And Eddie in those

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:32.790
days, referred to themself as a
transvestite. And in a stand up

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:37.500
routine that we had people view
at differentiated between weirdo

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:41.310
transvestites and executive
transvestites, and he does this

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:44.070
whole thing about it being a
broader community. That's

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:49.050
category work. That is exactly
the kind of benefit that contact

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:54.450
help provides. Our research has
been out there for a while now.

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:58.020
It's been replicated by scholars
literally all over the world,

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:02.010
working on mediated contact
between a wide variety of

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:05.490
majority and minority
population. So as an empirical

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:09.450
proposition, it's it's, it's in
pretty good shape. So what does

